Overview
Social media have deeply integrated into the lives of millions of people for a wide variety of purposes. What are social media? What are some possible implications of social media use? How can we improve interactions and experiences on social media? This course tries to provide answers to these questions. In particular, in this course, you will learn important concepts, terms, and theories related to social media, explore different social media sites, critically analyze possible social, political, and psychological impacts of social media use, and come up with ideas to adjust personal social media practices or design of social media. This course aims to prepare students for roles as social media researchers, designers, and users.

Learning Objectives
At the end of this course, a student should be able to:
1.	Understand important features of social media;
2.	Become familiar with prominent theories and research related to social media;
3.	Apply relevant theories, concepts and empirical research to explore social, political, and psychological implications of social media use;
4.	Conduct a research project independently, and 
5.	Provide suggestions to adjust personal social media practices or the design of social media.

This course is designed to help students achieve the following learning objectives:
1. acquire foundational knowledge of semantics and pragmatics;
2. develop critical thinking and logical reasoning skills for analyzing semantic issues;
3. learn the techniques necessary for conducting original research in semantics;
4. write a well-organized research paper in semantics.

This course introduces graduate students to research methodologies used in second language research. It covers quantitative and qualitative designs, mixed methods, and classroom-based research. Students will learn about common data collection approaches and understand concepts about research validity, reliability, and ethics. They will also learn to read and critically evaluate published studies. Through lectures, readings, presentations, assignments, and in-class discussions, students will develop the ability to:
1.	read published research articles and critically evaluate them; 
2.	formulate researchable questions based on issues that interest them; 
3.	select appropriate research designs and data collection methods;  
4.	design their own research; 
5.	present their study and write up a research proposal following academic conventions.


